 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Nvidia comeback shocker 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Tomohiro Ohsumi/Getty Images Nvidia's stunning decision to reinstate CEO Jensen Huang sent the company's stock skyrocketing, yanked the broader market out of correction territory, and raised existential questions over the president's role in decision-making at the biggest company in the economy's most vital sector.

 Nvidia's board said it was satisfied with Huang's "candor and ability to lead" after a series of meetings, Axios' Emily Peck and Madison Mills report . 

 Huang said in a statement: "I am honored to return to Nvidia and lead one of the most consequential technology companies in the world at the beginning of a new industrial revolution." CFO Colette Kress, who had been serving as interim CEO, will return to her previous responsibilities. 🇨🇳 Huang's return comes 11 days after Nvidia announced his resignation, and 15 days after he admitted to an affair. U.S. national security agencies believe the affair was with a woman who is "believed to be a Chinese intelligence asset and who was also believed to be involved in possible illegal smuggling of Nvidia chips into China," the N.Y. Times reports . 

 Huang is set to testify publicly before Congress on Dec. 3 over his relationships and questions about Nvidia's business practices — including whether it did enough to stop its advanced AI chips from ending up in China. Images: Donald J. Trump/Truth Social 📺 President Trump said he was directly involved in Huang's resurrection, telling CNBC this morning that "my intervention was very important." ( Watch clip. )

 Trump later posted a meme (above) imagining Huang surviving the 2024 assassination attempt against the president, with the CEO holding a red bra as an Nvidia flag waves in the background. 📈 Nvidia shares are up 30% as of midafternoon.

 The tech-heavy Nasdaq is up 6.2%, while the S&amp;P 500 is up 4.6%. What's next: Huang may now be beyond reproach, Axios' Nathan Bomey writes. 

 Peter Jaworski, a business ethics professor at Georgetown University, tells Axios: "If he can get away with this, what constraints can the board put on his behavior?" Go deeper . 

 
 
